## Claim
Text: "UK Employees unable to work in tier 3 areas will get a combination of Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit, which will mean they get 80% of their wages."
Claim author: Boris Johnson
Claim date: October 21, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What were the specific rules, payment percentages, and employer contribution requirements of the UK government's Job Support Scheme for businesses forced to close under Tier 3 restrictions in October 2020?
Answer: Under the expanded Job Support Scheme announced in October 2020, the UK government committed to paying "two thirds of each employees’ salary (or 67%), up to a maximum of £2,100 a month" for businesses legally required to close. Regarding employer contributions, "employers will not be required to contribute towards wages and only asked to cover NICS and pension contributions," meaning around half of potential claims were expected to face no employer contribution. To be eligible under the scheme's rules, "employees must be off work for a minimum of seven consecutive days" and must have been employed with an RTI submission notifying payment to HMRC made on or before September 23, 2020.

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/government/news/job-support-scheme-expanded-to-firms-required-to-close-due-to-covid-restrictions

### Did the combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit mathematically guarantee that every UK employee unable to work in Tier 3 areas received at least 80% of their normal wages?
Answer: No, the combination of the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit did not mathematically guarantee that every employee would receive at least 80% of their wages. According to the search result, the JSS itself only paid "2/3 of their salary" (approximately 67%), and while the addition of Universal Credit meant that "for many the remaining drop in earnings will be covered by the welfare system - up to 90%," this was not a universal guarantee. Instead, the system was designed to be "more generous to those who need it most," meaning the final amount received was highly dependent on individual circumstances, such as rent and income level.

Source URL: https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rishi-sunak_i-wanted-to-take-a-moment-to-explain-how-activity-6722764120606687232-8D0w?trk=public_profile

### What were the eligibility criteria and capital limits for Universal Credit in the UK in October 2020, and how did these criteria and limits affect employees' ability to claim Universal Credit alongside the Job Support Scheme?
Answer: In October 2020, eligibility for Universal Credit (UC) alongside the Job Support Scheme depended on factors such as income level, whether the claimant had children, and their level of savings. Specifically, the amount of UC a worker was eligible for started "reducing once you have £6,000 in savings," and anyone with "£16,000 in savings will not qualify for any UC, regardless of any other factors." Consequently, higher-income workers and those with savings above these limits were ineligible for UC, meaning they could not use it to top up their income and would receive only the 67% provided by the Job Support Scheme, falling short of the 80% wage mark.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### Were there any waiting periods or administrative delays for receiving Universal Credit that would prevent UK employees from immediately receiving 80% of their wages when Tier 3 restrictions were imposed?
Answer: Yes, there was a significant waiting period for receiving Universal Credit that would prevent immediate payment. According to the source, claimants "may have to wait for around 5 weeks for your first payment" because Universal Credit is "assessed and paid in arrears, on a monthly basis." The first assessment period lasts one calendar month, and claimants "will usually receive your first payment 7 days after the end of your first assessment period," though they "may be able to get a Universal Credit advance if you are unable to manage during this period."

Source URL: https://www.understandinguniversalcredit.gov.uk/new-to-universal-credit/how-and-when-youll-be-paid/

### Did independent financial or policy analysis organizations, such as the Resolution Foundation or the Institute for Fiscal Studies, verify Boris Johnson's claim that Tier 3 workers would receive 80% of their wages through this combination?
Answer: No, independent organizations like the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) did not verify Boris Johnson's claim, but instead demonstrated that many workers would receive less than 80% of their wages. For example, analysis by the IFS showed that "a single person with no children who owns their own home and earns £11,000 a year, would be entitled to a bit of UC if they were put on the Job Support Scheme, but not much, so they would end up on 73% of their usual income."

Source URL: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147

### Did the UK government subsequently modify or replace the Job Support Scheme with an extension of the Coronavirus Job Retention Scheme (furlough) shortly after Boris Johnson's statement on October 21, 2020?
Answer: Yes. According to the search result, the Job Support Scheme, which was scheduled to run for six months starting November 1, 2020, "has been postponed due to the extension of the Job Retention Scheme" (furlough).

Source URL: https://www.sargent-disc.com/2020/09/29/covid-19-job-support-scheme/

### How did household income and partner earnings affect an individual UK employee's eligibility to receive Universal Credit to supplement Job Support Scheme payments to reach the 80% wage level?
Answer: According to the eligibility rules, an individual must make a joint claim for their household, and "how much you can get will depend on your partner’s income and savings, as well as your own." Furthermore, to be eligible for Universal Credit, the household must "have £16,000 or less in money, savings and investments," meaning that a partner's high earnings or household savings could reduce the amount of support received or disqualify the individual from receiving the supplement entirely.

Source URL: https://www.gov.uk/universal-credit/eligibility

## Final Judgement
### Summary of Key Insights
The retrieved evidence demonstrates that the combination of the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit (UC) did not guarantee that UK employees unable to work in Tier 3 areas would receive 80% of their wages. The JSS itself only covered 67% of an employee's salary. While UC could theoretically top up this income, eligibility was highly restricted by household income, partner earnings, and savings limits (with savings over £16,000 completely disqualifying applicants). Consequently, many workers would not qualify for UC at all, leaving them with only the 67% JSS payment. Furthermore, independent analysis by the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) showed that even some workers who did qualify for UC would still only receive around 73% of their normal income, failing to reach the 80% threshold.

### Conclusion
Based on the evidence, the claim that the combination of the Job Support Scheme and Universal Credit would mean affected employees get 80% of their wages is `refuted`. The 80% figure was not a guaranteed minimum; rather, many employees would receive significantly less—either because they were entirely ineligible for Universal Credit due to personal savings or partner earnings (leaving them with only the 67% JSS rate), or because the UC calculation still left them short of the 80% mark.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
This claim is refuted because the combination of the Job Support Scheme (JSS) and Universal Credit (UC) did not guarantee that affected employees would receive 80% of their wages. Under the expanded JSS, the government only committed to paying 67% of an employee's salary ([UK Government News](https://www.gov.uk/government/news/job-support-scheme-expanded-to-firms-required-to-close-due-to-covid-restrictions)). While UC could theoretically supplement this income, it was not a universal guarantee ([Rishi Sunak's LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/rishi-sunak_i-wanted-to-take-a-moment-to-explain-how-activity-6722764120606687232-8D0w?trk=public_profile)). Eligibility for UC was strictly limited by household income, partner earnings, and savings, with savings over £16,000 completely disqualifying applicants ([UK Government UC Eligibility](https://www.gov.uk/universal-credit/eligibility)). Consequently, many workers would only receive the 67% JSS rate, and analysis by the Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS) demonstrated that even some who qualified for UC would only receive about 73% of their normal income ([BBC News](https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/54633147)). Additionally, claimants faced a five-week waiting period for their first UC payment ([Understanding Universal Credit](https://www.understandinguniversalcredit.gov.uk/new-to-universal-credit/how-and-when-youll-be-paid/)), and the JSS itself was ultimately postponed ([Sargent Disc](https://www.sargent-disc.com/2020/09/29/covid-19-job-support-scheme/)).